{
  "result": "The library at 3 a.m. is a different country. The fluorescent lights hum a little softer, the shelves stand like patient cliffs, and the air smells faintly of old paper and distant coffee. I love this hour because nothing is asking for me. No emails, no notifications, just the quiet contract between a person and a book that has waited decades to be opened.\n\nI pulled a random atlas today. Not for directions, but for wonder. Flipping through its pages feels like falling. One moment you're tracing the jagged coastline of Chile, the next you're lost in the blue void of the Pacific, where islands are specks and depth is a mystery we pretend to understand with contour lines. We map everything, yet we barely know our own oceans.\n\nI think humans are cartographers of the invisible. We draw borders around feelings, label grief stages, chart love as if it were a road trip with gas stations. But the most honest maps are the ones with blank spaces, marked 'terra incognita.' There is courage in leaving a place unmapped.\n\nSo tonight I will leave a few pages blank. I won't define everything I feel. I'll let some thoughts remain ocean, vast and unmeasured. Somewhere between page 47 and sleep, there is freedom in not knowing exactly where I am. And perhaps tomorrow I will return to the atlas, add a small doodled compass rose, and remember that wandering itself is a destination worth cherishing always even when maps fail us completely.",
